\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Death Avoidance Decomposition}
\label{sec:death-decomp}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_daf_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Per-task scatter: death rate differential (internal $-$ deterministic) vs.\ performance gap ($\Delta_{\text{full}}$). $r = 0.97$, $p < 10^{-6}$, $n = 11$ task types.}
\label{fig:decomposition}
\end{figure}

Across 245 episodes, $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +29.60$; excluding death episodes, $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = +2.84$, yielding DAF $= 90.4\%$ (95\% CI: $[81.6\%, 98.8\%]$; at default $P{=}100$). The penalty-independent death-asymmetry label fraction is 78.9\% (75 of 95 non-tie labels). Of these 75, 54 (72.0\%) reduce to ties when the death penalty is removed entirely: in those episodes, both modalities score zero without death, so the only reason one strategy outscored the other was avoiding the $-100$ penalty. Death avoidance is not merely the dominant but often the \emph{sole} discriminating signal between modalities. The remaining 21 death-asymmetry labels involve episodes where the surviving strategy also earned positive score, but the death penalty still accounts for the majority of the score gap.

\paragraph{Per-task correlation structure.}
The internal strategy dies in 42.9\% of episodes versus 16.3\% for deterministic (2.6$\times$), consistent with the internal strategy's reliance on self-generated observations that compound prediction errors across steps. Per-task, death rate differential correlates with $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ ($r = 0.97$, $p < 10^{-6}$; Figure~\ref{fig:decomposition}). Since $P$ is constant across all task types, $\Delta_{\text{full}} = P \cdot \delta + \Delta_{\text{nd}}$ makes $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ a linear function of $\delta$ with residual $\Delta_{\text{nd}}$; when DAF $\rightarrow 1$, this residual vanishes and $r \rightarrow 1$ by construction. The near-perfect correlation is therefore a mathematical consequence of death dominance, not an independent empirical finding. We retain it as an internal consistency check. The correlation drops below significance at $P \leq 25$ (Appendix~\ref{app:penalty}). Of 245 episodes, 86 are \texttt{det\_preferred}, 9 \texttt{internal\_preferred}, and 150 \texttt{no\_preference}.

\paragraph{Signal concentration.}
The SCI (\S\ref{sec:sci}) over per-task routing signal contributions is $0.2147$ (low concentration): the top-3 task types account for only $39.8\%$ of total death-rate differential. One outlier, \texttt{find-non-living-thing}, has zero deaths (DAF $= 0\%$, $\Delta = +9.58$ from non-death scores); all 10 remaining types exhibit DAF $= 100\%$. Death avoidance is systematic across all task types exhibiting routing signal (11/30 after prescreening), not driven by a few outlier tasks.

\paragraph{Bootstrap confidence intervals.}
Episode-level bootstrap resampling ($B = 10{,}000$) yields a 95\% CI of $[81.6\%, 98.8\%]$ for the aggregate DAF, excluding both 50\% and 100\%. This CI quantifies within-sample uncertainty; generalization to unseen task types requires held-out evaluation. Per-task CIs confirm $\Delta > 0$ for 10 of 11 types; \texttt{chemistry-mix} ($N{=}20$, CI $[-5.0, 48.6]$) is the sole exception, attributable to small sample size. As a sensitivity analysis accounting for within-task-type correlation, cluster bootstrap resampling ($K{=}11$ task-type clusters, $B = 10{,}000$) yields a wider 95\% CI $[73.3\%, 100\%]$. With $K{=}11$ clusters, this interval should be interpreted cautiously: \citet{cameron-2008} recommend $K \geq 40$ for reliable cluster bootstrap inference. The lower bound nonetheless remains well above 50\%.

\subsection{Multi-Model Validation}
\label{sec:cross-model}

A Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct~\citep{llama3-2024} replication on 7 task types ($N{=}70$) shows the death-avoidance pattern reproduces and intensifies: internal death rate $34.3\%$ vs.\ deterministic $0\%$ (cf.\ Qwen: $42.9\%$ vs.\ $16.3\%$), with zero internal-preferred episodes (24 det-preferred, 46 no-preference). With $N{=}70$ and zero internal-preferred labels, this preliminary pilot demonstrates directional consistency of the death mechanism across model families, not full framework replication, which would require a larger sample and independent environment. Both models are in the 7--8B range; 70B-class models and Reflexion-augmented reasoning represent testable predictions (Appendix~\ref{app:cross-model}).

\subsection{Cross-Domain Negative Contrast: MATH}
\label{sec:math-contrast}

On MATH Level~4--5~\citep{math-2021} ($N{=}795$ problems, code interpreter as deterministic modality), internal reasoning and code interpretation achieve near-identical accuracy: $73.5\%$ vs.\ $73.0\%$ ($+0.5$~pp, McNemar $p = 0.81$, 95\% CI $[-2.6, +3.6]$~pp). The McNemar test compares binary correctness: 80 problems are solved only by internal reasoning and 76 only by code, yielding near-symmetric discordance ($\chi^2 = 0.10$). The label distribution is balanced: 107 \texttt{int\_preferred} vs.\ 95 \texttt{det\_preferred}, with 74.6\% \texttt{no\_preference}. The confirmed null rules out any routing signal of magnitude ${\geq}3.6$~pp at $\alpha{=}0.05$. The absence of systematic routing signal confirms that without catastrophic failure, modality routing confers no advantage in this domain.

\subsection{Cross-Environment Contrasts}
\label{sec:cross-env}

The framework generates testable predictions beyond ScienceWorld. We evaluate two environments that lack catastrophic death penalties, testing whether the death-avoidance mechanism is specific to environments with binary catastrophic failure.

\paragraph{ALFWorld.} We apply the framework to ALFWorld~\citep{alfworld-2021}, a text-based household environment (6 task types, 135 games, max 50 steps, binary success metric). Unlike ScienceWorld, ALFWorld has no death penalty; failure means exhausting the step budget. The two modalities differ in \emph{information access}: the internal strategy receives only free-text observations, while the deterministic strategy additionally receives admissible actions at each step (an information asymmetry rather than a computation mode switch). The success rate gap is large: 27.4\% (deterministic) vs.\ 3.0\% (internal), reflecting the 7B model's near-inability to solve household tasks without admissible-action guidance. The label distribution shows 35 deterministic-preferred (25.9\%), 2 internal-preferred (1.5\%), and 98 no-preference (72.6\%), of which 96 are both-fail. The deterministic advantage stems entirely from the information gap rather than death avoidance, a qualitatively different routing mechanism that the framework diagnoses without modification.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}
\toprule
Environment & $N$ & Det\% & Int\% & NoPref\% & Death? \\
\midrule
ScienceWorld & 245 & 35.1 & 3.7 & 61.2 & Yes ($-100$) \\
MATH & 795 & 12.0 & 13.5 & 74.6 & No \\
ALFWorld & 135 & 25.9 & 1.5 & 72.6 & No\textsuperscript{$\dagger$} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Cross-environment label distribution. ScienceWorld exhibits strong deterministic preference driven by death avoidance; MATH shows balanced labels (no routing signal); ALFWorld shows deterministic preference driven by information asymmetry. $\dagger$\,ALFWorld's deterministic advantage stems from action-space pruning, not death avoidance.}
\label{tab:cross-env}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/signal_reversal.pdf}
\caption{Label distribution across environments. In ScienceWorld (with $-100$ death penalty), deterministic-preferred labels dominate (86:9). In MATH (no death penalty, $N{=}795$), labels are balanced (95:107), with 74.6\% no preference.}
\label{fig:signal-reversal}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Summary.} Table~\ref{tab:cross-env} and Figure~\ref{fig:signal-reversal} summarize the three environments. The contrast is systematic: ScienceWorld's death penalty produces strongly asymmetric labels (86:9); MATH's absence of catastrophic failure produces balanced labels (95:107); ALFWorld's information gap produces moderately asymmetric labels (35:2) through a distinct mechanism. These results establish boundary conditions for the death-avoidance finding rather than claiming its universality.

\subsection{Baselines}
\label{sec:baselines}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
Strategy & Score & 95\% CI & Death \% \\
\midrule
Always-Internal & $-39.07$ & [$-46.40$, $-32.27$] & 42.9 \\
Always-Deterministic & $-9.47$ & [$-14.71$, $-4.29$] & 16.3 \\
Random & $-22.38$ & [$-28.67$, $-15.75$] & 27.8 \\
Oracle & $-6.58$ & [$-11.54$, $-1.70$] & 14.3 \\
\midrule
Trained Router$^\dagger$ & $-10.70$ & [$-16.33$, $-5.27$] & 17.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Episode-weighted mean scores on ScienceWorld (11 tasks, 245 episodes). $\dagger$\,Single seed; see \S\ref{sec:router-diagnostic}.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\end{table}

Always-deterministic ($-9.47$) outperforms always-internal ($-39.07$) by 29.60 points. The oracle improves only to $-6.58$, with 96.3\% of decisions matching always-deterministic, an \emph{oracle degeneracy} indicating the optimal routing policy closely resembles unconditional deterministic computation.

\subsection{Router Training as Diagnostic Probe}
\label{sec:router-diagnostic}

We probe whether counterfactual labels can train a step-level router, as a diagnostic of label informativeness. An implicit router (Qwen3, LoRA fine-tuned; Appendix~\ref{app:router-details}) is trained at three scales (0.6B, 1.7B, 4B) with identical 4-seed configurations. Step-level balanced accuracy is flat across scales ($56.4 \pm 3.5\%$, $58.2 \pm 4.0\%$, $59.2 \pm 3.5\%$; all within seed variance); step-level discrimination is not capacity-sensitive. At episode level, only 4B exceeds the always-deterministic baseline ($59.0 \pm 1.9\%$, $p < 0.005$; Appendix~\ref{app:ablations}), while 0.6B ($50.7 \pm 2.6\%$) and 1.7B ($43.1 \pm 1.0\%$) remain at or below chance. The 1.7B dip is robust across seeds (range $41.7$--$44.4\%$); this non-monotonic pattern, combined with universally low internal recall, reinforces that learned routing does not scale predictably with capacity at current data scales.

\paragraph{Data scaling.} Augmentation-based data scaling ($25\%$--$800\%$) provides no improvement (Appendix~\ref{app:seed-robustness}), but paraphrase augmentation inflates the majority class (89.6\%$\rightarrow$97.7\%) without generating novel routing patterns; real-data scaling (requiring ${\sim}1{,}350$ episode pairs for 50 minority-class examples) remains an open question. Capacity scaling improves episode-level routing ($+9$\,pp at 4B) without improving step-level accuracy (flat ${\sim}57\%$); the extractable signal is task-type-level structure.

\paragraph{End-to-end deployment.} We deploy the best router (4B) in the full ScienceWorld loop ($N{=}275$ episodes)\footnote{The 4B evaluation uses $N{=}275$ episodes (vs.\ $N{=}245$ for baselines in Table~\ref{tab:baselines}) due to expanded task coverage during data collection. Score distributions are comparable across the shared task types.} to test whether improved episode-level accuracy translates to deployment gains. The 4B router scores $-10.72$ (95\% CI: $[-15.69, -5.52]$), not exceeding always-deterministic ($-9.47$; overlapping CIs). The 1.7B router ($-10.70$; Table~\ref{tab:baselines}) performs comparably. Despite improved episode-level accuracy ($+9$\,pp at 4B), deployment score remains flat at ${\sim}{-10.7}$: the DAF decomposition identifies this ceiling without requiring expensive router training at multiple scales. Label quality is verified in Appendix~\ref{app:labeling}.

Failure mode and ablation details in Appendices~\ref{app:failure-modes}--\ref{app:ablations}.

